{
  "abstract": "Let x_n = tan(arctan 1 + arctan 2 + ⋯ + arctan n) = B_n / A_n, where (1 + i)(1 + 2i)⋯(1 + ni) = A_n + B_n i in the Gaussian integers. Amdeberhan, Medina and Moll (2008) conjectured that x_n is never an integer for n ≥ 5. Ono (arXiv:2607.05739, Corollary 1.2) proved that there is an n₀ such that x_n is not an integer for every n ≥ n₀ outside a sparse exceptional set E = {n ≥ 5 : |x_n| > n/2 + 1}, without giving a value of n₀. This formalization proves, in Lean 4 with Mathlib and only the standard axioms, that n₀ = 5 works: for every n ≥ 5 with n ∉ E, A_n does not divide B_n. The proof combines a reduction (two distinct primes p > 2n dividing 1 + k² for some k ≤ n force |x_n| ≥ 2n + 2 at an integer value), twelve kernel-checked prime certificates covering 5 ≤ n ≤ 499,950,001,250, and a fully formal Chebyshev-type argument for all larger n. It does not prove the Amdeberhan–Medina–Moll conjecture: the indices in E are not treated, and E is not known to be finite.",
